::endsay

::lines intro
Good evening from Coldwater and welcome to CLUSTER PUCK. I'm Tug Brennan, and nobody made me do this.
CPhL hockey tonight, brought to you by whoever is still paying, and I have been told not to name them on air.
Evening. Tug Brennan with you. The scheduler says these two are playing, so these two are playing.
Welcome in. The ice is down, the lights are on, and the insurance has lapsed. Let's go.
::endlines

::lines matchup
Tonight the {away} come to town to face the {home}.
It's the {away} against the {home}, and one of these buildings empties out happy.
{away} at {home}. Somebody's night gets worse in the next hour.
The {away} in from out of district to meet the {home}. Sixty minutes, whatever's left of it.
::endlines

# ── rivalry night ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every club has exactly one rival (derived from the league in sports/hockey.js).
# The sim really does make these games nastier — more penalties, more fights — so
# these lines are reporting a fact, not selling a storyline.
::lines rivalry.intro
Evening, Tug Brennan with you, and it's a rivalry night. The medics have a second stretcher parked by the gate.
Good evening from Coldwater. These two clubs can't stand each other, and the officials have been told to keep their whistles handy.
Welcome in. When these two meet, the penalty minutes run about half again what they do on any other night.
::endlines

::lines rivalry.matchup
{away} at {home}, the meanest pairing on the schedule. The gloves come off nearly twice as often when these two meet.
The {away} and the {home}. Both benches have dressed their heavyweights tonight.
{away} into the {home}'s barn. The crowd has been on its feet since the warm-up, mostly shouting at the visitors' bench.
{away} versus {home}. The officials gave both captains a talk at the morning skate. Nobody listened last time either.
::endlines

::lines matchup.records
The {away}, {awayRecord} on the season, into the barn against the {home} at {homeRecord}.
On the marquee: {away} ({awayRecord}) versus {home} ({homeRecord}). Somebody's record gets worse tonight.
{away} bring {awayRecord} in here. {home} sit at {homeRecord}. The scheduler doesn't care.
::endlines

# ── the injury report ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Injuries PERSIST. A man carried off is gone for a stretch of games, and his club
# dresses somebody up from the reserve until he's back — so this is read before the
# opening face-off whenever anyone is missing. {missing} are the absent, {callups}
# the men in their sweaters tonight, {count} how many.
::lines injury.report
Before we drop it: {count} out of the lineup tonight. {missing}, all still on the shelf from what was done to them, and up from the reserve, {callups}.
The injury list, and it's long: {missing}. Dressing instead: {callups}. Best of luck to them, sincerely, they'll need it.
{missing} not in the building tonight, or not in a state to be. {callups} get the sweaters. Nobody has asked them if they want them.
No {player} tonight, still out, and the club brings up {callup}. This league doesn't do get-well cards.
{count} missing between these two. That's the CPhL in the back half of a season: eventually everybody is somebody's replacement.
::endlines

# ── periods ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines section.start
Here comes the {sectionOrd} period. Both teams are back out.
The {sectionOrd} period, and the linesman is skating the puck out to centre.
Back for the {sectionOrd}. The ice is wet where the Zamboni got to.
::endlines

::lines section.end
That's the {sectionOrd}. {home} {homeScore}, {away} {awayScore}.
Horn goes on the {sectionOrd}. {away} {awayScore}, {home} {homeScore}, and the Zamboni comes out to hide the evidence.
End of the {sectionOrd}. They'll resurface it, which is optimistic.
::endlines

# ── the drop ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There are nine dots on the sheet and the sim picks the one the RULE picks, so
# these three pools are not interchangeable — a centre-ice draw means somebody
# just scored or a period just started, an end-zone draw means the puck died in
# that end, and a neutral-zone draw is everything else. {winner} took it, {loser}
# lost it, {winTeam} has the puck, {zone} is where in plain English.
::lines faceoff.center
Back to centre ice. {winner} takes the draw from {loser} and the {winTeam} have it.
Centre-ice draw, and {winner} wins it clean. {winTeam} puck.
They line it up at centre. {winner} beats {loser} to it and away we go.
Puck's down at centre. {winTeam} come away with it.
::endlines

::lines faceoff.zone
Draw in the {zone}. {winner} wins it back for the {winTeam}.
Down in the {zone}, and {winner} takes it off {loser}.
They set up in the {zone}. {winner} gets a stick on it first and it's {winTeam} possession.
{winner} wins the draw deep. That's {foAway}–{foHome} on the dot tonight, if anybody's counting. Nobody is.
::endlines

::lines faceoff.neutral
Neutral-zone draw. {winner} ties up {loser} and the {winTeam} come away with it.
Dropped outside the line, and {winner} wins it.
{winner} takes the neutral-zone draw. Nothing much happens here and that's the point.
::endlines

::lines faceoff
Puck's down at the dot.
Clean draw, and away we go.
Won back cleanly, and the {winTeam} have it.
::endlines

# ── the intermission ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Fifteen minutes of dead ice between periods, and a broadcast that skips them cuts
# from a horn straight into a faceoff. Tug reads the period back: the scoring
# summary first (the only part somebody who left the room needs), then the shot
# clock and the penalty count, then whatever is left of whoever went off.
# {shotsAway}/{shotsHome}/{shots}, {penalties}, {fights}, {hits} are that PERIOD's
# numbers, counted from the beats — never the whole game's.
::lines intermission.intro
End of the {sectionOrd}, and they'll be fifteen minutes resurfacing what's left of it. Let's go back through it.
That's the horn. While they scrape it, here's your {sectionOrd} period.
Intermission. {away} {awayScore}, {home} {homeScore}, and here's how we got there.
::endlines

::lines intermission.summary
Scoring summary, {sectionOrd} period:
Your goals in the {sectionOrd}:
Here's the scoring:
::endlines

# One line PER GOAL, in the order they went in.
::lines intermission.goal
  {clock}: {shooter}, {team}. Assist: {assist}.
  {team}, {shooter} at {clock}, set up by {assist}.
  {shooter} for the {team}, {clock} on the clock. {assist} with the helper.
::endlines

# The same, for a goal nobody set up.
::lines intermission.goal.solo
  {clock}: {shooter}, {team}. Unassisted.
  {team}, {shooter} at {clock}, on his own.
  {shooter} for the {team}, {clock} on the clock. Nobody else touched it.
::endlines

::lines intermission.scoreless
No goals in the {sectionOrd}. Forty minutes of hockey between two clubs and nothing to show anyone for it.
Scoreless period. Both goaltenders held up, which in this league mostly means both of them are still conscious.
Nobody scored. I'd call it a goaltending duel but I watched it and it wasn't.
::endlines

::lines intermission.stats
Shots in the period: {away} {shotsAway}, {home} {shotsHome}. {penalties} minor infractions called, which is fewer than were committed.
{shots} shots between them in that period: {shotsAway} for the {away}, {shotsHome} for the {home}. The officials found {penalties} penalties in there somewhere.
By the numbers: {shotsAway}–{shotsHome} on goal, {penalties} to the box, {hits} hits that I'd describe as deliberate.
Shot clock says {away} {shotsAway}, {home} {shotsHome}. There were {fights} fights, which is under the league average, which is a sentence I hate saying.
::endlines

::lines intermission.casualties
Off the ice this period and not coming back: {casualties}. Their side plays the rest of this short. That's the rule and nobody's ever changed it.
{count} man off in that period. {player} in particular isn't going to be right for a while.
We lost {casualties} in there. No replacements. That's how a game gets away from a club in this league.
::endlines

::lines intermission.outro
Back in a moment for the {nextOrd}. Don't go anywhere. Statistically, something's about to happen to somebody.
That's your intermission. They'll be out shortly, most of them.
We'll take a break. When we come back there'll be fresh ice and the same problems.
::endlines

# ── chances ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines shot.save
{goalie} takes it in the chest and holds on.
{shooter} tests him and {goalie} says no.
Shot away, and {goalie} has it. Nothing doing.
Straight into the logo. {goalie} barely moved.
::endlines

::lines shot.glove
{goalie} SNAPS the glove shut on it. He didn't need to look.
Glove save, {goalie}, and he holds it up like he's proud of it. He should be.
{shooter} picks a corner and {goalie} picks it right out of the air.
::endlines

::lines shot.pad
Off the pad and into the corner. {goalie} kicks it away.
{goalie} gets a leg on it. That's all it takes.
Pad save. {shooter} will want that one again.
::endlines

::lines shot.blocked
Blocked. Somebody in a {team} sweater is going to feel that in the morning.
Down goes a defender in front and it never gets through.
{shooter} winds up and a shin gets in the way. That's the job.
::endlines

::lines shot.wide
Wide, and it rings around the boards.
{shooter} misses the net entirely. The glass takes it.
Off the mark. That one's going in the third row.
::endlines

::lines shot.post
IRON! {shooter} rings it off the post and the whole building makes a noise I'd describe as grief.
OFF THE POST. Half an inch. Half an inch, and {goalie} knows it.
CLANG. That's the sound of a man's night changing and not changing at the same time.
::endlines

::lines shot.breakaway
{shooter} is GONE, nothing between him and {goalie}, and {goalie} stones him.
Breakaway! {shooter} in alone, and he can't finish it.
Loose in the neutral zone, {shooter} is away, one on one, and {goalie} wins it.
::endlines

# ── goals ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines goal
{shooter} SCORES. {team} lead the noise, {home} {homeScore}, {away} {awayScore}.
IN. {shooter} beats {goalie}, and the horn does what the horn does.
{shooter} buries it. {assist} with the pass, and this building has come apart.
He shoots. HE SCORES. {shooter}, from {assist}, and {goalie} never had it.
::endlines

# A goal with no assist. The two lines above that don't name a helper are here too.
::lines goal.solo
{shooter} SCORES. {team} lead the noise, {home} {homeScore}, {away} {awayScore}.
IN. {shooter} beats {goalie}, and the horn does what the horn does.
He shoots. HE SCORES. {shooter}, all by himself, and {goalie} never had it.
{shooter} does it alone. Buries it, and this building has come apart.
::endlines

::lines goal.pp
On the power play, {shooter} makes them pay for it. Man advantage, man in the net.
{team} cash the power play. {shooter} with the finish. That penalty cost exactly what penalties cost.
Extra man, extra goal. {shooter}, and {goalie} was hung out to dry.
::endlines

::lines goal.sh
SHORTHANDED! Down a man and {shooter} goes the other way and scores. Someone is getting shouted at.
A shorthanded goal. The {team} are a man DOWN and they've just gone ahead on it.
{shooter} scores while his teammate sits in the box. That's the most embarrassing goal in hockey.
::endlines

::lines goal.en
Empty net, and {shooter} buries it from his own blue line. {home} {homeScore}, {away} {awayScore}. Turn the lights off on your way out.
Into the empty cage. {shooter}. That's the gamble, and it didn't pay.
{shooter} finds the empty net. The goalie is on the bench watching it happen from sixty feet.
::endlines

::lines hattrick
That's THREE for {player}. A hat trick in the CPhL, and nobody in this building owns a hat.
{player} completes the hat trick. Whatever they throw on the ice tonight, don't touch it.
Three goals, {player}. Remember the name, because at this rate he may not be with us long.
::endlines

::lines score.update
{home} {homeScore}, {away} {awayScore}.
That makes it {away} {awayScore}, {home} {homeScore}.
Scoreboard: {home} {homeScore}, {away} {awayScore}, {clock} left in the {sectionOrd}.
Your score, {away} {awayScore} and {home} {homeScore}.
::endlines

# ── officials ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines penalty
{player} goes for {infraction}. {penaltyMin} minutes, and the {team} are down a man.
Arm's up. {infraction} on {player}. {penaltyMin} in the box.
That's {penaltyMin} for {infraction}. {player} has a sit down and a think.
{player} caught for {infraction}. The officials noticed that one, which isn't guaranteed.
::endlines

::lines penalty.major
{penaltyMin} minutes for {infraction}. That's a major, and {player} is done thinking about it.
A five for {infraction} on {player}. Even these officials couldn't let that go.
{player} gets {penaltyMin} for {infraction}. He knew what he was doing and he did it anyway.
::endlines

::lines powerplay.start
{team} go to the power play.
Man advantage {team} for the next two minutes. Let's see if they can do anything with it.
Power play, {team}. This is where games get decided and hearts get broken.
::endlines

::lines powerplay.kill
Killed it off. Back to even strength.
Penalty killed, and the {team} bench is very pleased with itself.
They survive it. Back to five a side.
::endlines

# ── violence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines penalty.fight
Gloves off. {fighters} have decided the scoreboard isn't settling this. Linesmen are giving it a count of five before they pretend to care.
And they go. {fighters}, right at centre ice, and nobody is in a hurry to stop it.
Here it is. {fighters}, sweaters up over the heads, and the building is on its feet.
::endlines

::lines fight.knockdown
{winner} lands the big one and {loser} goes down flat. The linesmen step in now that it's safe.
A right hand, and {loser} is on the ice looking at the lights. {winner} stands over him for a while.
{loser} goes down in stages. {winner} helps him the rest of the way.
::endlines

::lines fight.jersey
{winner} has the sweater over {loser}'s head and is just teeing off now. {loser} can't see where they're coming from.
Sweater over the head, and {loser} is fighting blind. {winner} empties the tank into the back of it.
{winner} yanks the jersey up over {loser}'s helmet and keeps swinging. That's the oldest move there is.
::endlines

::lines fight.linesmen
The linesmen finally get in between them, {winner} still swinging over the top.
They're pulled apart. {winner} got the better of that one by a distance, and {loser} knows it.
Linesmen step in and haul them off each other. {loser} is bleeding from somewhere, {winner} from nowhere.
::endlines

::lines fight.result
{winner} takes it. {loser} takes five, and the {winnerTeam} go on the power play for winning a fistfight, which is the CPhL rulebook in one sentence.
It's {winner}. {loser} is helped up and sent away, and his team pays for it with a man.
{winner} wins the fight and wins his side the man advantage. Whoever wrote these rules wasn't well.
::endlines

::lines boards
{hitter} finishes {victim} into the boards and the glass takes the rest.
RIDDEN INTO THE BOARDS. {hitter} on {victim}, and that whole pane is still moving.
{victim} gets caught with his head down and {hitter} puts him through the dasher.
Big hit, {hitter}. {victim} will get up from that one. Probably.
::endlines

::lines boards.glass
{hitter} puts {victim} into the glass at full speed, and the glass very nearly lets him through.
THE GLASS. {hitter} on {victim}, and the people in the front row just got a good look at his face.
{victim} goes into the pane head first courtesy of {hitter}. That's a crack they'll be looking at all period.
::endlines

::lines boards.behind
From behind. {hitter} runs {victim} face first into the boards, and nobody in stripes saw a thing.
{victim} never saw {hitter} coming, because {hitter} came from behind. Numbers to the boards and straight in.
That's a hit from behind, {hitter} on {victim}, and the dasher has his face on it now.
::endlines

::lines boards.hip
{hitter} drops the hip and {victim} goes right over the top of him, skates in the air.
HIP CHECK. {victim} upended by {hitter}, cartwheels, lands on his neck. The building loves it.
{victim} cuts to the middle and {hitter} hips him into next week. Feet over head.
::endlines

::lines boards.elbow
Elbow up, and {hitter} leaves it in {victim}'s face on the way past.
{hitter} leads with the elbow and {victim}'s head goes back into the boards. An assault anywhere else. Legal here.
That's an elbow, high, {hitter} on {victim}, and the helmet's going one way and {victim} the other.
::endlines

::lines scrum
Bodies in front now, sticks up, everybody shoving and nobody swinging.
It's a scrum in the corner. Four men, one puck, no dignity.
They're all in there kicking at it. This is most of hockey, if we're honest.
::endlines

::lines injury.wound
That's {wound}, by the look of how he's holding himself.
The trainer's calling it {wound} before he's even off the ice.
Word from the bench is {wound}. He'll be on the shelf a while.
::endlines

::lines injury.sever
And {wound} isn't attached to him any more. It's over by the boards.
That's {wound}, lying a few feet from {player}. They're bringing out the stretcher and a bag.
{player} has left {wound} on the ice. The trainer picks it up and follows the stretcher off.
::endlines

::lines injury
{player} isn't getting up. He's helped off, and the {teamName} are down to {remaining} skaters, no replacement, that's the rule, and they wear it for the rest of the night.
That's {player}'s evening finished. {teamName} play the rest of this short.
{player} goes down the tunnel under his own power, barely. {remaining} left on the bench and nobody coming over the boards to help.
::endlines

::lines death.wound
{wound}. Nobody needs the doctor to tell them.
{wound}. They're putting a towel over him now.
{wound}. The building has gone completely quiet.
::endlines

::lines death
{player} IS DEAD. That's the game: sudden death, and the CPhL has never pretended it meant anything else. {winnerTeam} win it.
He's gone. {player}, on the ice, in overtime, and that's how this league ends a tie.
{player} doesn't get up, and he isn't going to. {winnerTeam} take it. Somebody call his people.
::endlines

# ── late game ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines pull
{teamName} pull the goalie. Down {down}, six on five, and the net is empty behind them.
Here comes the goalie to the bench. {teamName} going for it with the extra attacker.
Empty net at the far end. {teamName} are down {down} and out of patience.
::endlines

::lines ot.intro
Level after sixty. SUDDEN DEATH, and in this league that isn't a figure of speech.
We go to overtime. First goal wins it. So does the first body.
Sixty minutes settled nothing, so now we find out the other way.
::endlines

::lines shootout.intro
Nobody died and nobody scored, so we're going to the shootout. Frankly it's a let-down.
To the shootout. One at a time, one on one, and the crowd hates it.
::endlines

::lines shootout.score
{shooter} scores. {soAway}–{soHome} in the shootout.
Buries it. {shooter} makes no mistake.
::endlines

::lines shootout.miss
{goalie} turns aside {shooter}. Still {soAway}–{soHome}.
No good. {shooter} tries to be clever and isn't.
::endlines

::lines shootout.winner
{winner} take the shootout {soAway}–{soHome} and the two points.
It's over. {winner} win it in the skills competition, which counts, apparently.
::endlines

# ── colour ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines chatter
The ice in here hasn't been resurfaced since the ownership change, and you can tell; {team} are skating through what I'd generously call a puddle with ambition.
Goal judge's been in that booth thirty years. Nobody's checked on him in twelve. Light still works.
{goalie} has the thousand-yard stare of a man watching his own future arrive on a two-on-one.
The Zamboni runs on something the league won't disclose and the driver wears a respirator. Draw your own conclusions.
Nobody founded this league. The scheduler just started sending pairings one morning and the clubs turned up. Forty years later here we all are.
That's the third time tonight someone's gone head first into the boards and the third time the officials have looked at their shoes.
They sell a hot dog here that's legally not a hot dog. There's a sign. Nobody reads the sign.
I'm told the trophy is a pre-Handoff vessel of unknown industrial purpose. They fill it. They drink from it. Champions do tend to die.
The {team} bench is quiet. Then again half of them are hurt and one of them is, I believe, technically deceased.
There's a man in section nine who has come to every home game for nineteen years and has never cheered.
Attendance tonight is announced as capacity, which it always is, because nobody counts.
Somewhere in this building is a room with all the league's records in it. Nobody has ever found the door.
The glass behind that net has been replaced four times this season. Same pane. Same corner. Same reason.
{shooter} came up through the Ashway. You can tell by the way he skates like the ice owes him money.
It's fourteen below out there and colder in here, which is a choice somebody made deliberately.
The CPhL doesn't have a commissioner. It has a scheduler, and the scheduler has never been late.
::endlines

# ── the barns ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Booth colour for a specific HOME building. The narrator looks for
# `chatter.<first word of the home club's name>` and falls back to the general
# `chatter` pool, so authoring a barn is entirely optional — a club with nothing
# written for it behaves exactly as it did before. Add more as clubs earn them.

::lines chatter.waterfront
You can hear the cranes through the roof here. Third period, low tide, the whole building hums.
The Waterfront barn floods twice a winter. They play through it. There's a line painted on the wall showing how high, and it's above my head.
Half this crowd came straight off a shift, and half of them are still in their hard hats. The hats aren't for the hockey.
The Wharf Rats count the rats under the stands as season ticket holders. On a quiet night they outnumber the crowd.
Somebody throws a fish on the ice after every Rats goal. Nobody has ever seen who. The fish is always fresh.
::endlines

::lines chatter.district
The Goons' lobby has a gun rack. Coats and firearms, same counter, same attendant, one ticket.
The crowd here doesn't cheer. It gets quieter, and every visiting club will tell you that's worse.
District 9 numbers its streets and not its buildings. The away bus parks with its engine running.
Most of the Goons carry a badge on a weekday. The rest handed theirs in, or had it taken off them.
The Goons lift in the Precinct 9 gym after the night shift. The prisoners get the bench back in the morning.
Nobody's ever pressed charges over a Goons hit. Nobody's sure who they'd press them with.
::endlines

::lines chatter.westside
The Glasshouse barn has a climate curtain over every door and the coldest ice in the league. The seats are heated.
Season tickets at Westside are inherited. There's a waiting list, and it's mostly lawyers.
Westside warm up to a string quartet. Same four players for eleven years. They wear earplugs for the third period.
::endlines

::lines chatter.boomtown
The Boomtown barn was a foundry. Some nights you can still smell the casting sand.
Every Bulldogs home game starts with the anvil. A man in an apron hits it once and the building goes quiet.
The rail along the boards here is pipe from the old works, painted orange. It rings when somebody goes into it.
::endlines

::lines chatter.marquee
The Marquee barn shares a wall with a nightclub. Third period, you can feel the bass through the glass.
Half the crowd at the Marquee came out in costume for something else. They stay for the fights.
The scoreboard here used to be a cinema sign. Two of its letters have been missing for forty years.
::endlines

::lines chatter.coldwater
The Highlanders walk out to a piper. He isn't good. He's played every home game for fourteen years.
Marrow Street pulls its shutters down an hour before puck drop. Everybody on it is in here.
The Highlanders' sweaters are woven on a loom above a chip shop on Marrow Street. It's a real clan sett, the Malcolm.
::endlines

# A barn's pool is keyed by the first word of the club's name, so the St Garneau
# Guillotines' is chatter.st and the Yard Birds' is chatter.yard.
::lines chatter.st
The Guillotines are Father Jerome's club. He blesses the ice before the drop, and the ice crew waits until he's done.
The home bench at St Garneau's is two pews out of the nave, bolted to the boards.
Every Guillotines sweater goes through the Sisters of St Garneau's laundry on a Monday. The stains mostly come out.
Father Jerome never misses a home game. He sits by the door, and he watches it more than the ice.
::endlines

::lines chatter.yard
The Yards barn is a cold store with seats bolted in. The ice was here first.
The tannoy here came out of a freight shed. In the second intermission it still reads out bay numbers.
Birds fans crow when their side scores. All of them at once, and it goes on longer than you'd think.
Somebody gets a live rooster into this barn every season. Security has stopped asking how, and nobody has caught the rooster.
::endlines

::lines final
FINAL from Coldwater: {home} {homeScore}, {away} {awayScore}. {leader} take it.
That's the game. {leader} win it, {homeScore} to {awayScore}, and everyone still standing gets to go home.
Final score, {away} {awayScore} and {home} {homeScore}. {leader} with the win.
Horn goes. {leader} take it {homeScore}–{awayScore}, and the cleanup crew earn theirs tonight.
::endlines

::lines outro
That's CLUSTER PUCK. I'm Tug Brennan. Mind how you go, and mind what's on the ice.
For the CPhL, I'm Tug Brennan. There's another one tomorrow, because there's always another one.
Tug Brennan, saying goodnight from Coldwater. Nobody won anything permanent.
::endlines

# ── the Coldwater Cup ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines cup.intro
This is it. The Coldwater Cup. Nobody can produce a record of who commissioned it, and the one reporter who went looking took a job in Terminus instead.
Cup final tonight. One game, one trophy, and a trophy nobody can explain.
Cup final at the Slush Fund, the league's own barn out by the south wall. The ice belongs to the league as well, and it shows.
::endlines

::lines cup.matchup
For the Coldwater Cup: the {away} against the {home}.
{away} and {home}, for the Cup. Whatever it originally held, it holds champagne tonight.
::endlines

::lines cup.final
THE {leader} HAVE WON THE COLDWATER CUP. {home} {homeScore}, {away} {awayScore}. They'll drink out of it, because that's the tradition, and we'll see who's still here next season.
IT'S OVER. The {leader} are your Coldwater Cup champions. Carve it into whatever's still standing.
::endlines

::lines cup.outro
Coldwater Cup champions, the {leader}. Enjoy it. Historically, that has been the advice.
The {leader} take the Cup. I'm Tug Brennan, and I'd let it air out before you drink from it.
::endlines

# ── the call ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Short lines that follow the play as the rink shows it: the rush touch by touch,
# the shot, the save, the draw, the hit, each punch. Most are under eight words so a
# screen reader finishes one before the next arrives. Each one airs at the moment
# the picture does the thing it says.
#
# Written the way a booth calls it: who, where, what. {where} is where the touch is
# ("at the left point", "in the slot", "on the right wall", "through centre"),
# worked out from the same keyframe the rink plays it at, with left and right as the
# carrier skates; {from} is the same for the shot ("from the slot"). A pass is never
# just a pass twice running: it's fed, threaded, feathered, saucered, chipped, banked.
# The shot says what it was and the save says how it was stopped. The goal is one
# burst and the crowd does the rest. A word in CAPS is the one the voice leans on.

::lines pbp.breakout
{player} gathers it in his own end.
{player} with it, heading up.
{player} turns it up ice.
{player} picks it up deep and goes.
{player} starts it out.
Puck to {player}, own end.
{player} carries it out.
{player} motors up the ice.
::endlines

::lines pbp.pass
{player} to {to}.
{player} feathers it to {to}.
{player} threads it to {to} {where}.
Across to {to} {where}.
{player} slides it over to {to}.
{player} finds {to} {where}.
{player} hits {to} in stride.
{player} ladles it to {to}.
{player} saucers one over to {to}.
{player} chips it ahead to {to}.
Back to {to} {where}.
Tape to tape, {to}.
{player} drops it for {to}.
{player} banks it off the wall to {to}.
::endlines

::lines pbp.entry
{player} gains the line.
{player} brings it in {where}.
{player} carries it over the blue.
{player} steps into the zone.
{player} walks it in {where}.
{player} beats his man to the line.
Over the line, {player}.
::endlines

::lines pbp.cycle
{player} {where}.
{player} with it {where}.
{player} works it {where}.
{player} protects it {where}.
{player} holds it {where}.
{player} carries it {where}.
::endlines

::lines pbp.deke
{player} dangles one {where}.
{player} goes around his man.
Toe drag, {player}. Gone.
{player} undresses a defender {where}.
{player} fakes the shot and walks him.
{player} spins off a check.
{player} leaves a man sitting on the ice.
::endlines

::lines pbp.dump
{player} dumps it in.
{player} chips it deep.
{player} rims it around the boards.
In behind them, {player} chasing.
{player} flips it in and goes after it.
::endlines

::lines pbp.battle
Loose puck {where}.
It's free {where}.
Two men after it {where}.
Scramble {where}.
Nobody has it.
Sticks and elbows {where}.
::endlines

::lines pbp.won
{player} comes out with it.
{player} wins the race.
{player} digs it free {where}.
{player} out of the pile with it.
{player} wants it more.
{player} pitchforks it loose.
::endlines

::lines pbp.shot
{shooter} shoots!
{shooter} lets it go {from}.
{shooter}, the {shotLabel}!
{shooter} fires {from}.
Shot, {shooter}!
{shooter} lets fly.
::endlines

::lines pbp.shot.slap
{shooter} winds up.
{shooter} loads up {from}.
Big windup, {shooter}!
{shooter} leans into a slapper.
{shooter} hammers one {from}.
{shooter} with the cannon.
::endlines

::lines pbp.shot.wrist
{shooter} snaps a wrister.
{shooter}, quick wrist shot {from}.
Wrister from {shooter}.
{shooter} pulls it in and shoots.
{shooter} drags it and fires.
